WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.680
We ended the previous video with a few questions.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.040
Let me recall them for you.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:13.340
How do we calculate the mass of a single atom?

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.920
Is it practically possible to do so?

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.840
Is there any weighing balance designed to weigh

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.560
the mass of a single atom?

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.060
Let us now find out the answers to these questions.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.500
Imagine we have two objects A and B.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.700
Object A weighs around 12 kilograms is what we know.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.240
But we do not know the weight of object B

00:00:35.245 --> 00:00:37.260
and that's what we need to find.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.440
How do we find that out?

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.060
We just have a weighing balance with us.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.120
Let's put them on either side of the balance and see what happens.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.300
Clearly, object A is heavier.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.960
Now if we want to find the weight of B,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.920
we will need something smaller to compare it to.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.895
So let's say we cut object A into 6 equal pieces.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.620
And now we compare the weight of object B with one piece.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.200
We see that this piece is still

00:01:05.205 --> 00:01:07.225
heavier than the object B.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.660
This probably tells us that we need a smaller piece for comparison.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.560
So let's assume we cut object A

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.260
into 12 equal pieces,

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.340
which means each piece weighs one kilogram.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.200
And now when we place them on either side of the balance,

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.500
we see that it's in equilibrium.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.240
Now can we tell the weight of the object B?

00:01:28.485 --> 00:01:31.175
Yes, each piece of object A here

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.540
is one kilogram.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:37.120
And hence we can say that object B also weighs one kilogram.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.620
Let me ask you something now.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.095
Can we say that the mass of object B is one twelfth

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:44.880
that of object A?

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:46.200
That's correct.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.960
This is exactly how we estimate the atomic mass of an atom.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.080
Since we cannot directly calculate

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.560
the mass of an atom,

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.240
we usually calculate it with

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.800
respect to the mass of a carbon atom.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.840
We take carbon-12 as our standard.

00:02:01.175 --> 00:02:03.855
Wait, what is carbon-12?

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.400
What does it mean?

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:08.000
The element carbon has many isotopes

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.680
and the carbon-12 is one among them.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.480
And do we know what isotopes are?

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.040
Well, for now, we need to understand that they are different forms

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.600
of the same element.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:20.320
So in the case of carbon,

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:23.020
we will have slight differences in the atoms

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:25.380
which is what makes them isotopes.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:28.675
So carbon-12 is one such isotope which is

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.300
found almost everywhere in nature.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.100
Thus, an atom of carbon-12

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.240
is considered to be a standard unit

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:39.440
and atomic mass is thus calculated with respect to

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.260
one atomic unit.

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:44.300
And how do we define one atomic unit?

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.515
One atomic mass unit is defined as the

00:02:47.515 --> 00:02:50.575
mass unit exactly equal to one twelfth of the

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.180
mass of a carbon-12 atom.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:57.200
So let us assume this is one atom of carbon-12.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.700
We divide this in 12 exact equal pieces.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.640
We take one piece from this out of the complete unit.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:07.060
Can we say the mass of this single piece

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:08.960
is one atomic mass unit?

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:10.660
Absolutely!

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:12.760
And this is used as a standard

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:16.100
to calculate the atomic mass of any other atom.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.620
Some examples of this would include the

00:03:18.625 --> 00:03:21.235
atomic mass unit of hydrogen being 1,

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.460
carbon being 12, nitrogen being 14,

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:27.300
oxygen being 16 and so on.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.780
How do we understand this in a simple way?

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.860
Let me ask you a question.

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.340
How do you measure the mass of a person?

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:37.360
Using kilograms, right?

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:40.860
We say that this person's mass is 50 kilograms.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.800
Similarly, we say that the atomic mass of oxygen

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.680
is 16 atomic mass units or 16 amu.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:52.640
This is how we measure the atomic mass of any atom.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.700
The units used are 'atomic mass units'.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.680
But do we know what exactly gives the atom its mass?

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:01.480
I mean, what exactly is

00:04:01.485 --> 00:04:04.065
responsible for giving the tiny atom some

00:04:04.065 --> 00:04:05.260
amount of weight?

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.580
Let us know more about the concept

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.340
of atomic mass in the next video.

